# SYNOPSIS

```usage
printf format [arg ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`printf` writes <format> on standard output, each conversion in it replaced by the next <arg> as C's `printf` would format it.
It writes no newline of its own.
When the arguments outlast <format>, <format> is used again, until all of them are; a conversion with no argument left is given the empty string, or 0.
A <format> that converts nothing is written once.

<format> may hold the escapes `\\`, `\a`, `\b`, `\e`, `\f`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\v`, `\"`, `\'`, `\`<ddd>, one to three octal digits, and `\x`<hh>, one or two hexadecimal digits.
`\c` ends <format> there, and it is not used again.

A conversion is `%`, then any of the flags `#`, `-`, `+`, space and `0`, then a width, then `.` and a precision; the width or precision may be `*`, taken from the next <arg>.
`%%` is a `%`.
The conversions are:

: `%s`
  The <arg> as it is.
: `%b`
  The <arg> with its escapes, as <format>'s, replaced; a `\c` in it ends all output.
: `%c`
  The first character of <arg>, UTF-8.
: `%d`, `%i`, `%o`, `%u`, `%x`, `%X`
  The <arg> as an integer: decimal, octal with a leading `0`, or hexadecimal with a leading `0x`.
  An <arg> starting with `'` or `"` gives the code of the character after it.
: `%a`, `%A`, `%e`, `%E`, `%f`, `%F`, `%g`, `%G`
  The <arg> as a floating-point number.

`printf` takes no flags: its first argument is always <format>, even when it begins with `-`.

`printf` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/printf`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/printf`.
There is no native `printf`.

# EXAMPLES

As `tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs it in the POSIX namespace:

```text
% printf '%d %s %#x\n' 3 x 31
3 x 0x1f
```

